Challenge Overview

We have three files:

    userinfo.txt - Contains personal details of the target

    hash.txt - Contains SHA-1 hash of the password: 968c2349040273dd57dc4be7e238c5ac200ceac5

    check_password.py - Script to test passwords against the hash

Step-by-Step Solution
Step 1: Examine the Files

First, let's see what information we have:
bash

# Navigate to the directory
cd ~/Downloads

# Check the user information
cat userinfo.txt

Output:
text

First Name: Alice
Surname: Johnson
Nickname: AJ
Birthdate: 15-07-1990
Partner's Name: Bob
Child's Name: Charlie

bash

# Check the hash
cat hash.txt

Output:
text

968c2349040273dd57dc4be7e238c5ac200ceac5

bash

# Examine the checking script
cat check_password.py

The script reads passwords.txt and checks each password against the SHA-1 hash.
Step 2: Install CUPP (Common User Passwords Profiler)
bash

# Clone the CUPP repository
git clone https://github.com/Mebus/cupp.git

# Navigate into the CUPP directory
cd cupp

Step 3: Generate Custom Wordlist with CUPP

Run CUPP in interactive mode:
bash

python3 cupp.py -i

When prompted, enter the information from userinfo.txt:
text

> First Name: Alice
> Surname: Johnson
> Nickname: AJ
> Birthdate (DDMMYYYY): 15071990

> Partner's name: Bob
> Partner's nickname: (press Enter)
> Partner's birthdate (DDMMYYYY): (press Enter)

> Child's name: Charlie
> Child's nickname: (press Enter)
> Child's birthdate (DDMMYYYY): (press Enter)

> Pet's name: (press Enter)
> Company name: (press Enter)

> Do you want to add some key words about the victim? Y/[N]: n
> Do you want to add special chars at the end of words? Y/[N]: y
> Do you want to add some random numbers at the end of words? Y/[N]: y
> Leet mode? (i.e. leet = 1337) Y/[N]: y
> Hyperspeed Print? (Y/n) : n

CUPP will generate a wordlist file named alice.txt with approximately 29,372 words.
Step 4: Prepare the Wordlist for the Checking Script
bash

# Go back to the Downloads directory
cd ~/Downloads

# Copy the generated wordlist and rename it to passwords.txt
cp cupp/alice.txt passwords.txt

# Verify the wordlist was copied correctly
ls -la passwords.txt
wc -l passwords.txt  # Should show around 29372 lines

Step 5: Run the Password Checker
bash

# Execute the checking script
python3 check_password.py

The script will test all passwords in passwords.txt against the SHA-1 hash. This may take a minute or two.
Step 6: If Password Not Found - Try Alternative Approaches

If the script doesn't find the password, try these additional steps:
Option A: Run CUPP with Different Settings
bash

cd ~/Downloads/cupp
python3 cupp.py -i
# This time, answer 'n' to Leet mode and special chars

